---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `local first = "Ada"
local last = "Lovelace"

print(first .. " " .. last)
print(#first)
print(first:upper())
print(last:lower())`;
const Figure1Out = `Ada Lovelace
3
ADA
lovelace`;

const Figure2 = `local unit = "reactor"

print(string.upper(unit))
print(unit:upper())`;
const Figure2Out = `REACTOR
REACTOR`;

const Figure3 = `local unit = "Reactor"
local charge = 98

print(unit .. " is at " .. charge .. " percent")
print(string.format("%s is at %d%% charge", unit, charge))`;
const Figure3Out = `Reactor is at 98 percent
Reactor is at 98% charge`;

const Figure4 = `local code = "SKYNEST"

print(code:sub(1, 3))
print(code:sub(-4))
print(code:sub(2, 2))
print(code:find("NEST"))`;
const Figure4Out = `SKY
NEST
K
4\t7`;

const Figure5 = `local banner = [[
+------------------+
|  SKYNEST  v1.0   |
+------------------+]]

print(banner)`;
const Figure5Out = `+------------------+
|  SKYNEST  v1.0   |
+------------------+`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua - Skynest crew badge

local first = "Ada"
local last = "Lovelace"

local fullName = first .. " " .. last
print(fullName)
print(#fullName)`;
const Task1Out = `Ada Lovelace
12`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua - Skynest crew badge

local first = "Ada"
local last = "Lovelace"
local role = "engineer"
local charge = 98

local fullName = first .. " " .. last
print(fullName)
print(#fullName)

print(string.format("%s (%s) - reactor at %d%%", fullName, role:upper(), charge))`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua - Skynest crew badge

local first = "Ada"
local last = "Lovelace"
local role = "engineer"
local charge = 98

local fullName = first .. " " .. last
print(fullName)
print(#fullName)

print(string.format("%s (%s) - reactor at %d%%", fullName, role:upper(), charge))

local code = last:sub(1, 3):upper() .. "-" .. first:sub(1, 1):upper()
print("Badge ID:", code)`;

const Checkpoint = `Ada Lovelace
12
Ada Lovelace (ENGINEER) - reactor at 98%
Badge ID:\tLOV-A`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.3: Working with Strings</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Three things about Lua strings will surprise you if you have written
            JavaScript or Python. <b>You join them with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code=".."
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            rather than{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="+"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, you measure them with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="#"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            rather than a length property, and they are counted from 1.</b>
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Lua uses a separate operator for joining because{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="+"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is reserved for arithmetic and nothing else. If you have ever been
            bitten by JavaScript quietly turning{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`"16" + 1`}
              lang={"js"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            into{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`"161"`}
              lang={"js"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, this is the design that prevents it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">That colon</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="first:upper()"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is shorthand. Every string function really lives in a library called{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="string"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and the colon just passes the string in as the first argument.
            These two lines are the same line.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure2Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Worth filing away: <b class="text-sn-theme">the colon is not a
            string feature.</b> It is a general Lua mechanism, and in Activity
            1.11 you will use the same syntax to build your own objects.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Building a sentence</h3>

          <p>
            Chaining{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code=".."
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            works, and numbers convert themselves along the way. It also gets
            ugly fast, which is what{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="string.format"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is for.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure3Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            The placeholders are{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="%s"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            for a string and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="%d"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            for a whole number, filled in by the arguments that follow, in
            order. To print an actual percent sign you write{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="%%"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, since a lone{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="%"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            means a placeholder is coming.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Taking a string apart</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="sub"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            cuts out a piece, given a start and an end position.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Both ends are included, and counting
            starts at 1</b> &mdash; not 0. A negative number counts back from
            the end.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure4}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure4Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            That last line returns <i>two</i> values &mdash; where the match
            starts and where it ends. Lua functions are allowed to hand back
            more than one thing, which is unusual and extremely handy. Activity
            1.7 covers it properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The 1-based counting is not a string quirk.</b> Everything in Lua
            that has positions counts from 1, including tables. Getting used to
            it here makes Activity 1.9 much less startling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Strings that span lines</h3>

          <p>
            Double square brackets hold a string exactly as you typed it, line
            breaks and all, with no escaping.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure5}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />
          <Code
            code={Figure5Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            A line break immediately after the opening{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="[["
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            is skipped, which is why the banner above does not start with a
            blank line.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Start a fresh <b class="text-sn-theme">main.lua</b>. You are
            building a crew badge line, the kind of thing a game would print
            above a character.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Join and measure</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Store a first name and a last name in separate variables.</li>
            <li>
              Join them into a <b>fullName</b> variable with a space between.
            </li>
            <li>Print the full name, then print its length.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <Code
            code={Task1Out}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Format a status line</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Add variables for a role and a charge percentage.</li>
            <li>
              Use{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="string.format"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              to print one line containing all three.
            </li>
            <li>
              Put the role in capitals, and end with a real percent sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "5-6, 12" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Generate a badge ID</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Build a code from the <b>first three letters of the last name</b>,
              a hyphen, and the <b>first letter of the first name</b>.
            </li>
            <li>Make the whole thing uppercase.</li>
            <li>
              You can chain calls &mdash;{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="last:sub(1, 3):upper()"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              works, because{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="sub"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              hands back a string and a string has an{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="upper"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "14-15" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Print a boxed banner above the badge using a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="[[ ]]"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              string.
            </li>
            <li>
              Change the badge so it uses the <b>last</b> letter of the first
              name instead of the first. Use a negative position rather than
              counting the length yourself.
            </li>
            <li>
              Try{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code={`print(#"café")`}
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and see whether you get what you expected.{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="#"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              counts <i>bytes</i>, and some characters take more than one.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>Your finished file should produce this.</p>

          <Code
            code={Checkpoint}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            title="Output"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            If your badge came out as <b>LOV-A</b> without you counting a single
            character by hand, you have the idea. If it came out one letter
            short or long, you have met the 1-based counting for real, which is
            a better way to learn it than being told.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a class="btn btn-outline-light" href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-2">
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-1/1-4">
            Next: Numbers and Math <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
